.TH KLVTOOL-RECORD 1 "1970-01-01" "klvtool dev" "User Commands"
.SH "NAME"
klvtool\-record \- Capture inbound source bytes to a file with no decoding.
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
klvtool record \-\-input <path\-or\-url> \-\-out <path> [\-\-duration <dur>] [\-\-idle\-timeout <dur>] [\-\-max\-bytes <N>] [\-\-record\-overwrite] [\-\-header "K: V"] [\-\-iface <name>]
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
Capture bytes from a path or URL source to a regular file with no MPEG\-TS demuxing, PSI parsing, or KLV decoding involved.
.PP
`record` is the streaming counterpart to letting another tool dump a file: it understands udp://, tcp://, http(s)://, rtsp://, srt://, and '\-' (stdin) the same way the other streaming\-aware commands do, and it accepts the shared `\-\-duration`, `\-\-idle\-timeout`, and `\-\-max\-bytes` stop conditions. The captured file can be replayed later with the file\-based commands.
.PP
Use `\-\-input file.ts` to copy a regular file (useful for normalization after sync\-byte recovery).
.SH "OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-\-duration\fR \fI(default 0s)\fR
stop after this wall\-clock duration (e.g. 30s, 5m, 1h); 0 disables
.TP
\fB\-\-header\fR
extra HTTP request header in 'Key: Value' form (repeatable; e.g. \-header "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN"). HTTPS only — RTSP servers must embed credentials in the URL until token\-auth is wired up.
.TP
\fB\-\-idle\-timeout\fR \fI(default 0s)\fR
stop if no bytes are written for this duration; 0 disables
.TP
\fB\-\-iface\fR
egress network interface for UDP multicast joins (Linux: device name like eth0, or a local IP)
.TP
\fB\-\-input\fR
input source path or URL (file://, udp://, tcp://, http(s)://, rtsp://, srt://, or '\-' for stdin)
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-bytes\fR
stop after at least this many bytes have been captured (record only; may overshoot by up to one I/O buffer); 0 disables
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-packets\fR
stop after this many TS packets are observed; 0 disables
.TP
\fB\-\-out\fR
destination file for captured bytes (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-record\fR
tee inbound source bytes to this file (works for any \-\-input, including a regular file)
.TP
\fB\-\-record\-overwrite\fR
allow \-\-record to overwrite an existing file (default refuses; honors O_CREATE|O_EXCL semantics)
.SH "EXAMPLES"
Capture 30 seconds of a multicast UDP feed
.RS 4
.nf
$ klvtool record --input "udp://239.0.0.1:5000?iface=eth0" --out cap.ts --duration 30s
.fi
.RE
.PP
Capture from a Bearer\-token authenticated HTTPS source
.RS 4
.nf
$ klvtool record --input https://relay/stream --out cap.ts --header "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" --duration 1m
.fi
.RE
.PP
Pipe ffmpeg's MPEG\-TS output through klvtool for byte capture
.RS 4
.nf
$ ffmpeg -i source.flv -c copy -f mpegts - | klvtool record --input - --out cap.ts --max-bytes 104857600
.fi
.RE
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
\fB0\fR
clean stop — captured bytes through duration / idle / max\-bytes / SIGINT / EOF
.TP
\fB1\fR
source open failure, destination write failure, or stream interrupted mid\-run
.TP
\fB2\fR
invalid usage (missing flags, malformed URL)
.SH "ENVIRONMENT"
.TP
\fBNO_COLOR\fR
disable ANSI color in any error output
.SH "SEE ALSO"
\fBklvtool\fR(1),
\fBklvtool\-decode\fR(1),
\fBklvtool\-inspect\fR(1)
